<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kosoko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1872</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kosoko was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-lagos">Oba of Lagos</a> from 1845 to 1851. He seized the throne from his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akitoye">Akitoye</a> and protected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> great profit. In December 1851 a British naval attack, the Reduction of Lagos, drove him out and restored Akitoye; Kosoko withdrew to Epe and was later allowed to return to Lagos, where he died in 1872.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kosoko.jpg" alt="Kosoko" width="320" height="632" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Openworks · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Kosoko_(1845-1851).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
